  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTo simply put it, it finally feels like fall this afternoon. Winds \nstill remain on the breezy side, especially across western Minnesota \nwhere gusts between 20-25 mph are being reported. These winds are \nremnant from a post-mid-level wave which is now located over \nsouthern Ontario. Winds will decrease this evening once this wave \ndistances itself away from the northern plains. Skies for the rest \nof this afternoon should remain clear although some clouds will \nbuild from the west this evening. Afternoon highs expected to reach \nthe mid-60s to lower 70s and lows overnight falling to lower 50s and \nsome upper 40s north and east of I-94.\n\nOn Sunday, an upper-level trough will move east across Alberta into \nOntario. The apex of this trough is forecast to sink as far south as \nsouthern Minnesota. Forcing is expected to be rather weak however \nits possible that a few scattered rain showers develop mainly south \nof the MN River Valley. However, brought slight chance PoP values up \ninto portions of southern MN for any rouge precip that wants \ndevelop. By Sunday afternoon, a 1020mb sfc high builds in over the \nwestern Great Lakes region allowing for skies to clear with \nafternoon with highs near 70 degrees.\n\nOur eyes remain fixed on Monday's forecast. There is still \nfavorable disagreement between model membership for total QPF. \nFor example, the EPS still holds as the wetter solution. If this\nsystem develops in favor of EPS trends, an inch of rain could \nfall as far north as the Twin Cities metro and closer to 1.5 \ninches across southern and southeastern Minnesota. On the other \nside of the coin, the GEFS remains incredibly pessimistic. Much \nof the northern half of Minnesota including the Twin Cities \nmetro could see closer to 0.25\" while southeastern MN would see \nnear 0.75\". For now, decided to remain with NBM QPF amounts \nwhich would provide 1-1.5 inches from a line south of Ladysmith,\nWI down to Fairmont, MN. Areas northwest of this line would see\nbetween 0.5-1.0\" of rainfall.\n\nTo make sense of the model disagreement, a break down of both the \nEPS and GEFS can help paint the picture. Aloft, both models have a \nnegatively tilted trough that digs in over southern Saskatchewan\nallowing for favorable forcing to be in place over the northern\nplains. However, the EPS features a tighter pressure gradient \ncompared to the GEFS. In the lower-to-mid levels, both models \nfavor strong warm moisture advection which clearly supports the\nincreased rainfall chances. At the surface, beneath the \nnegatively tilted trough aloft, the GEFS depicts 998mb surface \nlow while the EPS shares the same feature but stronger at \n992mb. Now if we factor in the GEPS (Canadian Ensemble), this \nfavors closer to the result of what the GEFS is suggesting. \nAnother element to be aware of is the low-end severe weather \npotential mainly along the I-90 corridor. the upper-level wave \nproducing this system could make for a narrow window of wind-\nshear of about 30kts. The thermodynamic profiles are not \nimpressive but with Low LCLs and combined shear, we cannot turn \nour head away from a small tornado threat Monday evening. In the\nend, the QPF forecast remains highly uncertain. Any shift in \ntrack could greatly decrease/increase QPF amounts for a given \narea. Of course, those who manage to see greater thunderstorm \nactivity will have a shot at observing locally higher amounts. \n\nBeyond Monday, much of the remaining forecast period is under zonal \nflow. Forecast highs will remain the the upper 60s to lower 70s. A \ncouple of additional rain chances will arrive late Wednesday and \nthen again on Friday.",
